"working_notes": "## Open questions\n\n- Pacific Sunrise: the Drive file name credits Cecile Leroy, the notes say Rodney taught it. The VOM packet doesn't include it.\n- Texas Sandy Hill: key C and form AABB' now come from the Strum Machine chord chart in Drive, and the recording ends on C. The title still matches nothing published. The notation PDF was only 15 KB and came back inline instead of as a file, so it wasn't rendered.\n- JAWBONE.mp3 in the main Drive folder doesn't count under the status rules (confirmed Sep 25). Jawbone stays not played yet.\n- Captain Campbell: confirmed as the strathspey, but its key is still unconfirmed. One Scottish country dance book has a Captain Campbell's Strathspey in A minor with three parts, which may be a different tune.\n- Tentative matches to confirm by ear: Valhalla = Vallafield.\n- They Took My Wife Away Last Night: likely the Gaelic air/strathspey Ghoid iad mo bhean uam an raoir (\"They Stole My Wife Last Night\"). Tunearch's 1784 source is in A minor, but modern versions vary, so the key is still blank.\n- Cedar Paths: the VOM packet table of contents calls it Cedar Pass.\n- Polka overlap: irishtune.info lists Kerry Polka and Egan's as alternate titles of Peg Ryan's. Three rows may cover two tunes, or even one.\n- Booth Shot Lincoln: tunearch traces it to Marcus Martin, western North Carolina, not Kentucky. Origin not changed yet.\n- Trip to Skye: The Session and other sources list it as a waltz. It's still filed under Irish.\n- Reconciliation Reel: irishtune.info and tunearch list a Reconciliation reel in A major (also called The Olive Branch). The key stays Em? in case you heard a different tune.\n- Man of the House: irishtune.info gives it as a 16-bar single reel (AB). The existing AABB may just be how the session plays it.\n- Four tunes in the Drive folder 'Tunes for the future' are excluded on purpose: Cluck Old Hen, On the Danforth, Opossum's Tail, Bearen Dance.\n- Commonality ratings assume a national average. The PNW Scottish scene runs higher on McCusker and Fraser-adjacent modern tunes; Frank's Reel is already rated 8 on that basis. The 27 tunes identified on Sep 24–25 are unrated.\n\n## Next steps\n\n1. The titles left in the unidentified list have no leads under the spellings tried. A recording or a few notes of each is probably the only way forward.\n2. Remaining `?` keys are mostly VOM camp tunes (Back Home, Cedar Paths, Dragon Slayer, Game of Drones, Pacific Sunrise, Habas de Montes Torozos). They're in the 7.7 MB VOM tune packet in Drive; exporting those pages as a smaller PDF would unblock them.\n"
